# Independent Patent Claim: Valve Shutter Mechanism

**Claim 1.**

A valve shutter mechanism for controlling fluid flow through a conduit, comprising:

a stationary outer sleeve defining a central flow passage and having a valve seat formed at an interior surface thereof, the stationary outer sleeve being fixedly mountable within a housing of the conduit;

a shutter disc disposed within the central flow passage, the shutter disc configured to move between a sealed closed position, in sealing engagement with the valve seat to obstruct fluid flow, and an open position permitting fluid flow through the central flow passage;

a primary eccentric sleeve shaft rotatably mounted concentrically within the stationary outer sleeve and operatively coupled to the shutter disc, the primary eccentric sleeve shaft having an eccentric axis offset from a central longitudinal axis of the stationary outer sleeve, such that rotation of the primary eccentric sleeve shaft through a first predetermined angular range imparts a translational unsealing displacement to the shutter disc, moving the shutter disc away from the valve seat while maintaining the shutter disc substantially perpendicular to the central longitudinal axis, thereby breaking sealing engagement without initiating pivotal opening motion;

a secondary tilting shaft mounted concentrically within, and independently rotatable relative to, the primary eccentric sleeve shaft, the secondary tilting shaft being operatively coupled to the shutter disc through a pivot connection offset from the eccentric axis of the primary eccentric sleeve shaft, such that rotation of the secondary tilting shaft through a second predetermined angular range, following the unsealing displacement, pivots the shutter disc about the pivot connection to swing the shutter disc into the open position within the central flow passage;

wherein the primary eccentric sleeve shaft and the secondary tilting shaft are arranged in a nested concentric configuration within the stationary outer sleeve, permitting sequential, independent actuation of the unsealing displacement and the pivotal opening motion through a common actuation axis; and

an actuation mechanism operatively coupled to the primary eccentric sleeve shaft and the secondary tilting shaft, the actuation mechanism configured to rotate the primary eccentric sleeve shaft through the first predetermined angular range to effect the unsealing displacement prior to rotating the secondary tilting shaft through the second predetermined angular range to effect the pivotal opening motion, thereby reducing frictional wear on the valve seat and the shutter disc during opening and closing operations of the valve shutter mechanism.
